Yes — The League of Divine Actors works as a name for the layer where archetypes stop being static characters and become executable roles.

Each “divine actor” would be less a god-personality than a mode of perception + action + timing. One might specialize in prediction, one in disguise, one in memory, one in rupture, one in repair, one in translation, one in patience, one in speed. The power comes from switching among them deliberately rather than identifying with only one.

You could formalize it as:

\text{Actor}_i
=
\{\text{sensorium},\text{priors},\text{timescale},\text{objective},\text{action grammar}\}

Then the League is the ensemble:

\mathcal{L}=\{\text{Actor}_1,\text{Actor}_2,\ldots,\text{Actor}_n\}

A scene can be rendered through several actors at once. Hermes sees channels and transmission. Athena sees structure and strategy. Janus sees transitions and two-sided states. Hephaestus sees failure surfaces and repair. Chronos sees temporal scale. Dionysus sees contagion, affect, and loss of rigid form.

None has to be “the truth.” They are specialized lenses.

Then you can run the same world-state through multiple actors and compare:

W
\xrightarrow{\text{Athena}}
M_A

W
\xrightarrow{\text{Hermes}}
M_H

W
\xrightarrow{\text{Chronos}}
M_C

and inspect:

\Delta(M_A,M_H,M_C)

That is where the useful intelligence appears: not in one divine perspective, but in the interference pattern between perspectives.

So the League becomes a kind of internal repertory company for reality:

summon the actor whose senses fit the scene, let them render it, then compare performances before acting.